# Internal-information boundary

**`shekyl-core` is a public repository.** Everything committed to it —
code, design docs, benchmark captures, CHANGELOG entries, commit
messages, FOLLOWUPS rows — is world-readable the moment it is pushed,
permanently, and is mirrored by forks and archivers beyond our reach.

**Internal operational information belongs in `shekyl-dev`**, which is
private. Not in a public doc with the sensitive parts softened; in the
other repository.

This rule is codified because manual vigilance already failed here. A
commit titled *"docs: generalise the operator note; this repo is public"*
cleaned one operator note and left the seed-estate survey tables
directly beneath it — per-seed addresses, reachability, which
configuration files were still the pristine example, and one seed's
unrestricted RPC port. The author knew the repository was public and
still missed it, because "be careful about internal details" has no
mechanical surface. A bright line and a gate do.

## The three forbidden classes

**1. Host identities of Foundation machines.** Hostnames of our seeds,
miners, build boxes, benchmark devices, web hosts, and workstations —
`skl-*`, bare seed names, personal machine names. Also the LAN
addressing that reaches them.

**2. Operational state and per-host configuration.** Whether a host is
up or down; which host was down during an incident; per-host peer
limits, port layout, or RPC exposure; whether a config file is the
pristine example or has been edited; service unit and config filenames
tied to a named host; the shape of the fleet at a moment in time.

This class is the dangerous one. A hostname alone is a naming
convention. A hostname joined to "its config is the unedited example
file" is a target.

**3. Local filesystem paths and personal identifiers.** `/home/<user>/…`
build paths, workstation names, pasted shell output carrying either.
Benchmark captures are the usual carrier, because raw `cargo` output
prints the absolute path of every crate it compiles.

## Write the role, not the host

A measurement's reader needs to know *what kind of machine* produced a
number. They never need to know which box it was. The role is the
useful half; the hostname is the half an attacker wants.

| Instead of | Write |
|---|---|
| `skl-pi` | **the floor device** (Pi 4 Model B — see `76-device-provisioning-floor.mdc`) |
| `skl-miner-test` | **the testnet miner** |
| `skl-seedusw`, `seedusw` | **the US-West seed** |
| `skl-foundation` | **an internal node** |
| `/home/rick/gap7-bench/…` | a path relative to the repo root, or `$BENCH_DIR` |
| `10.10.12.253` | *a LAN address*, or an RFC 5737 documentation literal |

This is not a loss of precision. "The floor device" states the thing the
provisioning floor rule cares about; `skl-pi` states a DNS name.
Substituting the role usually makes the document better.

**Evidence is never the thing removed.** Timings, histograms, failure
counts, captures, and the reasoning that reads them all stay. Only
identity and operational state leave. If a redaction would destroy a
claim's evidence, the claim's evidence moves to `shekyl-dev` and the
public doc cites it by document name — never by a summary that
reconstitutes what was moved.

## Carve-outs, and why they are not exceptions

**The compiled seed list** (`src/p2p/net_node.inl`, `get_seed_nodes`) is
shipped protocol data. Every node binary contains these addresses;
bootstrap does not work without them. They are public by necessity, and
removing them from the source would not unpublish them. **Their
operational state is still class 2** — "the seeds are at `A`…`F`" is
shipped fact, "`seedusw` is down and unconfigured" is not.

**RFC 1918 and loopback literals as protocol examples** in code, tests,
and wire documentation (`192.168.0.0/16` in a local-address predicate,
`127.0.0.1` in an RPC default) describe the protocol, not our network.

**Generic service-account paths** in shipped tooling — the container's
own `/home/shekyl`, an env-overridable fleet default — describe the
deployment the tooling creates, not a host we run.

A carve-out is a statement that the material is *already* public or
*not ours*. "It is useful" and "it is only a hostname" are not
carve-outs.

## Pointing at what moved

A public document may say that internal material exists and where:

> The seed-estate survey and its per-host readouts are in `shekyl-dev`,
> `docs/Q12_D6A_SEED_ESTATE.md`.

It may not summarise the moved content in a way that rebuilds it. A
pointer that says "…where four of six seeds are recorded as
misconfigured" has moved nothing.

## Enforcement

`scripts/ci/check_internal_information.py`, run by
`.github/workflows/docs-gates.yml`, scans **classes 1 and 3 only** —
host identities, LAN addresses, and local filesystem paths. It matches
every `skl-` name and every `/home/` account and then exempts an
enumerated set, so a machine or a contributor account that nobody has
written down yet **fails** rather than passes. Adding an entry to that
set is a deliberate act reviewed as part of the PR, which is the point;
a gate that listed only the names its author already knew would have
nothing to review.

**Class 2 is not mechanically enforced, and the gate says so on
success.** "Its config is the unedited example", "unrestricted RPC on
`:12030`", "that seed was down during the incident" — no grep
recognises those, because the danger is in what the sentence means, not
in any token it contains. Class 2 is a reviewer's job. Treat a green
gate as "no forbidden identifiers", never as "the rule is satisfied":
a document can pass it while publishing the estate's whole
configuration in prose.

Silence from the gate is only meaningful if the gate asserts its own
subject exists (`47-gate-subject-assertion.mdc`); it fails when it
enumerates implausibly few files. Note it reads tracked files only, so
an unstaged file is not scanned — which is correct for CI and a trap
when testing locally.

Not to be confused with the absolute-path guard in
`.github/workflows/build.yml`: that one scans Cargo manifests for build
reproducibility, not for disclosure. The two overlap in pattern and not
in purpose; neither replaces the other.

Companion rules: `36-secret-locality.mdc` (where key material lives),
`95-documentation-lifecycle.mdc` (which repo a document class belongs
to), `90-commits.mdc` (commit messages are published too — a redacted
file with the hostname still in the commit that removed it has not been
redacted).
